1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,710
national enterprise architecture.

2
00:00:01,870 --> 00:00:04,390
Right. And you know, if you run these systems in your ministry,

3
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:08,810
you already know the technology is... Well, it's rarely the bottleneck.

4
00:00:09,270 --> 00:00:11,340
Yeah. The real friction is almost always political.

5
00:00:11,990 --> 00:00:20,770
So our mission today is to extract the four non-negotiable commitments you absolutely need from your minister to actually make this work.

6
00:00:20,910 --> 00:00:28,310
Exactly. We are specifically digging into the PAERA framework, which stands for the Public Administration Ecosystem

7
00:00:28,730 --> 00:00:29,970
Reference Architecture.

8
00:00:30,010 --> 00:00:32,130
Which I mean, sounds incredibly dense.

9
00:00:32,290 --> 00:00:37,710
It does, yeah. Yeah. But really just think of it as the master urban plan for your government's digital infrastructure.

10
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:45,140
It's the framework that prevents, you know, five different agencies from accidentally building five completely incompatible identity systems.

11
00:00:45,390 --> 00:00:50,510
Right. But you can have the best master plan in the world, and you still need planners to execute it.

12
00:00:50,530 --> 00:00:53,970
Which brings us to the first ask: you need a permanent EA team.

13
00:00:54,010 --> 00:00:58,470
We're talking two to four senior architects who report directly to your chief digital officer.

14
00:00:58,510 --> 00:01:02,670
Okay, wait. I wanna pause on that. Why the heavy emphasis on the word permanent?

15
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:09,440
Because ministers usually just prefer hiring, like outside project consultants for big digital pushes rarely.

16
00:01:09,510 --> 00:01:12,320
They do, yeah. Yeah. But consultants function like a, um,

17
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:18,070
a temporary construction crew. They arrive, they build a specific app, and then they leave.

18
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:18,710
Oh, I see.

19
00:01:19,030 --> 00:01:22,650
A permanent EA team acts as your actual city planners.

20
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:24,980
They are the institutional memory of your architecture.

21
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:30,380
Right. They maintain those cross-cutting standards whether a massive IT project is running or not.

22
00:01:30,410 --> 00:01:33,360
You simply cannot outsource your core digital sovereignty.

23
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:37,930
Exactly. Which leads us to the second ask in the PEO framework.

24
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:44,870
Mm-hmm. An EA board. Mm. And crucially, it needs absolute binding authority over major state registers.

25
00:01:45,050 --> 00:01:52,680
Now, see, I can hear ministers pushing back on this immediately saying something like, "Fine, we'll hire permanent planners, but let's just make them an advisory board so we don't step on any sector

26
00:01:52,810 --> 00:01:53,820
CIO's toes."

27
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:55,710
Right. That's the classic compromise.

28
00:01:55,770 --> 00:02:00,369
Yeah. So why is there such a strict insistence that the mandate has to be binding?

29
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,530
Doesn't that just create a bureaucratic choke point?

30
00:02:02,890 --> 00:02:11,190
Well, if you make this board purely advisory, agencies will just politely listen to the recommendations and then, you know, go buy whatever redundant software they wanted anyway.

31
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:12,450
Yeah, that happens all the time.

32
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:21,719
The EA board, which really needs to be chaired by the CDO or the minister directly, it has to be the mandatory checkpoint for every digital decision.

33
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:29,130
Without binding authority, your EA is just, um, an expensive PDF that nobody reads.

34
00:02:29,450 --> 00:02:33,850
So the mandate is what gives your architects the teeth they need to enforce the rules.

35
00:02:33,970 --> 00:02:37,410
Exactly. Okay, so we have permanent planners, and we've given them teeth.

36
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:39,939
The third ask is about funding them, right?

37
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:42,670
The briefs call for a five-year budget envelope.

38
00:02:42,850 --> 00:02:43,090
Yes,

39
00:02:43,810 --> 00:02:47,170
specifically representing about 2% of your total digital budget.

40
00:02:47,330 --> 00:02:52,520
But why a five-year lock-in? Why not just fund them annually like every other department in the ministry?

41
00:02:52,750 --> 00:02:57,510
Because annual line items disappear the moment a crisis hits or y- you know, the political winds shift.

42
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:02,110
Enterprise architecture is that quiet foundational governance overhead.

43
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:05,470
The stuff that makes the fast-moving projects actually run smoothly.

44
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:08,939
Right. So by protecting that 2% inside a five-year envelope,

45
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:13,530
you ensure the foundational work actually survives ministerial turnover.

46
00:03:13,570 --> 00:03:18,430
Which perfectly tees up the fourth ask, and honestly, this one reads more like a psychological contract.

47
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:24,710
It really is. You need a written promise that this EA team will not be pulled onto the urgent project of the week.

48
00:03:25,070 --> 00:03:26,990
I struggle to see how this works in reality, though.

49
00:03:27,270 --> 00:03:33,020
Like, if a flagship government app crashes, why wouldn't the minister pull their best architects into the trenches to go fix it?

50
00:03:33,210 --> 00:03:36,970
Because doing exactly that is the number one way EA programs die.

51
00:03:37,370 --> 00:03:42,450
Really? Yeah. When you pull your EA team into the trenches to write code for a temporary crisis,

52
00:03:43,070 --> 00:03:46,320
all the overarching architectural governance completely stops.

53
00:03:46,890 --> 00:03:47,950
The standards erode.

54
00:03:48,010 --> 00:03:51,430
Oh, wow. So ask four is essentially a political firewall.

55
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:56,830
It is. You have to get this protection in writing, or your EA program will just quietly die in its second year.

56
00:03:57,250 --> 00:04:07,079
And you need time for this to work. The briefs outline a horizon of, uh, six months to get a roadmap, 18 to 24 months to operate, and five full years for mature governance.

57
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:08,300
It takes discipline,

58
00:04:09,509 --> 00:04:11,370
but the global evidence proves it works.

59
00:04:12,090 --> 00:04:15,709
We have four very different governments using these exact four elements.

60
00:04:15,810 --> 00:04:19,830
Right. Starting with Rwanda, they have a small, strong center using the Orimbo platform.

61
00:04:20,050 --> 00:04:24,180
Then there's Kenya using their one-stop Huduma centers and the Huduma Namba system.

62
00:04:24,830 --> 00:04:28,130
Though the research notes Kenya Air's results are pretty mixed and openly debated.

63
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:31,350
Yeah, which usually points back to one of the four pillars slipping.

64
00:04:32,110 --> 00:04:37,890
Then you have South Africa, which coordinates federally through SITA using shared standards.

65
00:04:38,030 --> 00:04:39,470
And finally, Estonia.

66
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:46,820
They are highly mature, utilizing distributed registers on X-Road, which enables the once only principle.

67
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:50,650
Right, where citizens never have to give the government the same data twice.

68
00:04:51,210 --> 00:04:58,250
But here's what confuses me. Given that South Africa is federal and Rwanda is highly centralized, how do they use the exact same pattern?

69
00:04:58,590 --> 00:05:05,570
Well, despite the different sizes and constitutions, the foundational architecture relies entirely on having those same four elements securely in place.

70
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:09,130
The mechanics of adoption change, but the engine is identical.

71
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:12,310
A team, a board, a budget envelope, and a promise.

72
00:05:12,930 --> 00:05:17,510
You just need to put all four on a single page and ask your minister for the complete package.

73
00:05:17,890 --> 00:05:18,829
Exactly. Yeah.

74
00:05:18,930 --> 00:05:22,560
And you know, the minister who launches this work won't be the one who completes it.

75
00:05:23,310 --> 00:05:30,710
If your minister hears that five-year timeline and sees it as a feature rather than a problem, you've found the exact right leader.

